WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:04.440
Film Courage:&nbsp; Flawed character versus a
train wreck or is that what we want to see?

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:08.100
Peter Russell:&nbsp; Yeah, we want to see everybody
train wreck.&nbsp;

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.540
You want to see everybody in a room grappling
with their flaws.&nbsp;

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:17.970
I mean in a story like Madmen everybody had
a deep core wound.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:22.890
Peggy’s core wound is that she’s a woman
in a man’s world but she thinks like a man.

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:25.520
She was damaged deeply.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.720
Joan has deep core wound I’m only a sex
object, right?

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:31.759
And by the way, these didn’t heal right?

00:00:31.759 --> 00:00:37.190
At the end of the show Joan is still dealing
with the fact that she essentially whored

00:00:37.190 --> 00:00:43.450
herself out with this car executive and is
not taken seriously by men, yes?

00:00:43.450 --> 00:00:48.949
They never heal but it is the core wounds
that when they bump up against each other

00:00:48.949 --> 00:00:55.230
in the story that makes you wanna watch them
and audiences never need to know this.&nbsp;

00:00:55.230 --> 00:01:00.120
But if you ask yourself why a character is
interesting you’re looking at it’s almost

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.199
always because of that.&nbsp;

00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:04.100
How are they bleeding, right?

00:01:04.100 --> 00:01:09.170
The other thing done did because he was unlovable
was he made up an entire story about who he

00:01:09.170 --> 00:01:12.460
was he took someone else’s identity, yeah?&nbsp;

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:15.590
He took the identity of a guy who was killed
in Korea.&nbsp;

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:17.009
Again I’m not lovable.

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:19.060
I couldn’t be loved for me.&nbsp;

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:21.400
I gotta be somebody else.

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:25.380
So it’s always there in a great story.&nbsp;

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.020
There’s never a story that it’s not.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:32.500
Another spin that wasn’t true twenty years
ago but we become a more sophisticated audience

00:01:32.500 --> 00:01:33.930
in the United States.&nbsp;

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:41.560
We really do want depth of character that
we didn’t need twenty years ago and that’s

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:42.820
only going to continue.&nbsp;

00:01:42.820 --> 00:01:49.119
A story like Louie, which is a television
series with this great comic, it’s nothing

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:52.860
but him bleeding every week in surprising
ways.&nbsp;

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:58.970
Confessing things that sort of make our teeth
be set on edge but it’s compelling to watch

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:01.979
someone confess.&nbsp;

00:02:01.979 --> 00:02:03.960
Confession is riveting.&nbsp;

00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:07.780
Whenever I confess a personal wound of mine
in the classroom, everyone is talking and

00:02:07.780 --> 00:02:10.039
I’ve been talking a long time, everyone
just stops.&nbsp;

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:12.830
There is total silence in the room.&nbsp;

00:02:12.830 --> 00:02:16.110
They’re looking at me, they want to know.&nbsp;

00:02:16.110 --> 00:02:21.360
And this is why you can’t lie about your
core wound because that’s what people want

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:26.400
to know…”Oh…jeez…I can’t say the
truth…so..I’m going to making something

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:27.400
up….uh?

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.790
My mom molested me when I was four…?”

00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:31.550
You can’t make it up.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:36.849
Why because we have infinitely good bullshit
detectors as audiences.&nbsp;

00:02:36.849 --> 00:02:41.250
We will know I can always tell in a the story
when someone’s telling me something that

00:02:41.250 --> 00:02:42.930
isn’t true or isn’t their experience.&nbsp;

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:44.640
We know immediately.&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:50.200
You’ve got to tell the truth or audiences
will be bored.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:53.709
So whatever your pain is you can’t lie.&nbsp;

00:02:53.709 --> 00:02:57.550
It’s the truth that makes people want to
watch you.

00:02:57.550 --> 00:03:01.569
Film Courage:&nbsp; Why do we love confessions?

00:03:01.569 --> 00:03:05.569
Peter:&nbsp; Well….let me ask you.. what’s
your deepest most personal, embarrassing secret?

00:03:05.569 --> 00:03:08.860
Film Courage: I’ll tell you when the cameras
are off.&nbsp;

00:03:08.860 --> 00:03:10.510
There is more than one.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:16.400
Peter:&nbsp; Exactly….exactly but if you were
to tell me now I would love hearing it, why?

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.430
Film Courage:&nbsp; My only sense would be because
then you could measure your own (secrets/wounds)

00:03:20.430 --> 00:03:26.299
against what I’m telling you and then go
“I guess I’m not that bad after all.”

00:03:26.299 --> 00:03:29.760
Peter:&nbsp; Or,&nbsp; “I’m not alone.

00:03:29.760 --> 00:03:31.269
You’re in pain.

00:03:31.269 --> 00:03:33.319
So am I.&nbsp;

00:03:33.319 --> 00:03:35.860
Now I understand you.&nbsp;

00:03:35.860 --> 00:03:39.220
Now I empathize with you.&nbsp;

00:03:39.220 --> 00:03:41.939
Now you’re like me.&nbsp;

00:03:41.939 --> 00:03:43.739
Now it’s a story about me.&nbsp;

00:03:43.739 --> 00:03:50.390
It’s not some wonderful person who’s leading
a perfect life that I might fantasize about&nbsp;

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:58.250
and enjoy as a reality show, or a Kardashian
show,&nbsp; but it’s the deeper part of our

00:03:58.250 --> 00:04:01.000
lives which is “I’m in pain.

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.720
I don’t have everything I want.&nbsp;

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:04.720
Are you like me?

00:04:04.720 --> 00:04:06.860
Oh my God, you are now.&nbsp;

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:08.049
Thank you.&nbsp;

00:04:08.049 --> 00:04:09.460
Now I will follow you.

00:04:09.460 --> 00:04:12.980
Because if you can get better I can get better.&nbsp;

00:04:12.980 --> 00:04:16.680
Empathizing with the character is all about
confession.&nbsp;

00:04:16.680 --> 00:04:23.111
If you’ll notice in a straight story, confession
of your core wound or showing the core wound

00:04:23.111 --> 00:04:27.000
is something that happens almost immediately
in great stories.&nbsp;

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:33.220
You want to show the character’s wound and
his compensation often in the first 10 minutes

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:36.160
of the show and in a movie that’s certainly
true.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:42.640
The quicker you show it, the better the empathy
will be for the audience.

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:45.720
Don’t hide it.&nbsp;

00:04:45.720 --> 00:04:47.380
And every scene is about it.&nbsp;

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:51.760
Every scene is about the core wound and about
how you’re either curing it or making it

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:52.760
worse.&nbsp;

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:58.100
And every character in a movie is only ripping
at the wound or healing it.&nbsp;

00:04:58.100 --> 00:04:59.610
That’s all it is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.610 --> 00:05:07.360
Movies are just….think of a movie story
as a boxing ring and the hero jumps into the

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:09.660
ring but he’s bleeding.&nbsp;

00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:11.830
He starts out bleeding.

00:05:11.830 --> 00:05:16.760
And the whole movie is people jumping in with
bandages to help him heal or people jumping

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:19.780
in with knives to rip it wider open.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.780 --> 00:05:24.110
In a television story that just continues
for season after season.

00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:25.110
But it’s the same thing.

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:26.110
QUESTION FOR THE VIEWERS:&nbsp; What character,
movie or TV show does this conversation bring

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:26.112
to mind?

